arkets that view is  turned on it said the market are  characterized by market failure in  Europe the fixed access network market  is characterized by market failure in  every one of the 28 countries in the  European Union and that's why the fixed  access network market is regulated in  every one of those countries the problem  is that today you can make almost any  technology choice and it will be capable  of delivering the speed that  policymakers are looking for however if  you choose today and limit yourself what  will that help you with tomorrow  unfortunately when it comes to  technology choices policy makers say  it's none of the business and are  reluctant to get involved meanwhile the  dominant companies are free to choose  the technology that solves their  short-term problems but does not meet  the long-term aspiration of Europe  citizens to be clear the ftth Council  Europe is not asking policymakers to  specify the actual technology they would  like to see but we believe that they  should at a minimum specify the  performance characteristics of the  candidate technology in more detail  whether that is upload speeds service up  time or ability for the physical network  to be unbundled in other words can the  network be shared with rival operators  to encourage competition competition in  retail services the fact that  policymakers do not specify these  characteristics does not make sense in  the context of the European regulatory  framework to give Commissioner thing and  vice president answered credit they are  approaching at their task in a very open  and constructive way and they are  clearly seeking out the best way to  proceed for everyone in the industry  today let me say however I think it's  absolutely vital that we put consumers  at the heart of this discussion we know  that cuz  you miss would pay for high speed once  they understand what they can actually  do with those high speeds if we have  well informed consumers who are able to  choose fro